 "Students examine how schools, publishers, exams, dictionaries, and media enforce language norms."}, {"id": "bbda0c6d-808f-4412-a5bd-600629ec5064", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Usage controversies", "children": [{"id": "6b895b51-ba0b-4080-bb55-f69a28ff9bf7", "type": "concept", "title": "split infinitive"}, {"id": "477d6bae-f277-4361-a25e-3bafc8dde183", "type": "concept", "title": "singular they"}, {"id": "123507c9-b179-4a4a-8996-191e8b5dc57b", "type": "concept", "title": "who / whom"}, {"id": "bb1b1acb-a44b-48f5-b86a-be56cc9985ad", "type": "concept", "title": "double negative"}, {"id": "1b08fa1e-0eaf-493c-a248-1f0c094d317c", "type": "concept", "title": "passive voice"}, {"id": "6748486b-90e3-44f3-9186-97084638ebe0", "type": "concept", "title": "ending preposition"}], "description": "This subtopic treats familiar usage disputes as evidence for language ideology and change."}, {"id": "354ba0d8-5766-4fe7-bbd2-39fc8535dc32", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Corpus evidence and actual use", "children": [{"id": "5f299d22-8c5d-4df1-b995-8afb2ccebed3", "type": "concept", "title": "corpus linguistics"}, {"id": "4c59d65e-5738-47b8-9502-735327ea171d", "type": "concept", "title": "COCA"}, {"id": "d02bae14-1923-40c5-bfd5-cb6fe0d46025", "type": "concept", "title": "Google Ngram"}, {"id": "104c616c-70d9-4fcc-9952-accd7dffafff", "type": "concept", "title": "frequency"}, {"id": "72247bdc-e557-4e8a-bc43-482f8576996c", "type": "concept", "title": "collocation"}, {"id": "2371d0ef-f58c-47a4-b182-83e1ee4fd73b", "type": "concept", "title": "register"}], "description": "Students learn to compare prescriptions with real usage data."}]}, {"id": "2180e801-63c5-4bc2-a82f-b77e8158f36e", "type": "topic", "title": "World Englishes and Global English", "description": "English is no longer one national language. This unit studies English as a global, postcolonial, multilingual, and locally adapted language family.", "children": [{"id": "a910b16e-b9a9-4ef8-94f7-7e7d7bb4f438", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models of World Englishes", "children": [{"id": "2601a693-26ae-4b33-a662-48b293f9c17b", "type": "concept", "title": "Kachru's circles"}, {"id": "607eb3fd-7b66-417c-9c59-c6e616f99a0c", "type": "concept", "title": "inner circle"}, {"id": "f2be019f-ce7f-4399-9e58-d80ab7a2ebf6", "type": "concept", "title": "outer circle"}, {"id": "a56bf1bf-a0de-4226-8aff-924e5e906f56", "type": "concept", "title": "expanding circle"}, {"id": "2abc91b0-5acf-49d6-8e6a-7723ebc9b030", "type": "concept", "title": "pluricentric language"}, {"id": "caf3e60c-5e8f-448d-b0a4-bddf9d0c508d", "type": "concept", "title": "English as a lingua franca"}], "description": "Students learn frameworks for describing global English without treating all varieties as deviations from one center."}, {"id": "17ae70ce-3e51-4f1a-ba66-ad1ac30d76e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial and local Englishes", "children": [{"id": "df440912-4ba7-40f9-ab07-bd9fa6432c6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian English"}, {"id": "f7b59440-b363-459d-add3-b106313a2cb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Nigerian English"}, {"id": "c7fa2a93-d922-43eb-9c10-f08b3cb3edfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Singapore English"}, {"id": "70f4fefb-6246-4dd4-863c-57eefd144ffe", "type": "concept", "title": "Caribbean English"}, {"id": "046dd990-49a3-4c63-9a35-ce65436e6d00", "type": "concept", "title": "code-mixing"}, {"id": "e668fb33-ddf4-40d0-8061-d767b329dce5", "type": "concept", "title": "local norm"}], "description": "This subtopic examines English varieties shaped by colonial history, education, literature, and multilingual contact."}, {"id": "95dd6364-8742-4ab1-bb55-e9bf7ddd1718", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global English and power", "children": [{"id": "2e262321-d1b1-457e-9f8a-70728001d2b4", "type": "concept", "title": "linguistic imperialism"}, {"id": "3648595f-0450-4a01-9672-868fcc85417a", "type": "concept", "title": "language policy"}, {"id": "01eb43f7-b088-4737-91bd-e56881f51d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "accent prestige"}, {"id": "8dda4c3a-7d6f-4cb1-bca6-fcba15c017ad", "type": "concept", "title": "globalization"}, {"id": "4bacbaab-7fde-41b8-a7ee-16b3becfd5e3", "type": "concept", "title": "translation"}, {"id": "1aab6507-830d-490d-ab36-1eb89e712002", "type": "concept", "title": "language rights"}], "description": "Students analyze how English creates access, inequality, prestige, resistance, and identity."}]}, {"id": "8e393d85-21d5-450b-aefe-268eb51c0494", "type": "topic", "title": "Dialects, Variation, and Sociolinguistics", "description": "Sociolinguistics studies language variation as patterned, meaningful, and tied to identity, place, class, race, gender, and situation.", "children": [{"id": "7f702b76-e8a6-4d5e-9f5f-9bf750d49f7a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional and social dialects", "children": [{"id": "1c5fad93-72df-4b09-832a-4d593939b9fe", "type": "concept", "title": "dialect"}, {"id": "e4b4c7ad-5437-4da2-be2b-4e838d4e2406", "type": "concept", "title": "idiolect"}, {"id": "eda4c80d-a07b-4480-b561-e04763de9785", "type": "concept", "title": "isogloss"}, {"id": "741b23b7-1c8d-4a8f-9c91-7c6e7691d092", "type": "concept", "title": "regional variation"}, {"id": "067a682c-2f53-4097-9abe-7cbb00312c56", "type": "concept", "title": "social class"}, {"id": "4ed2a600-8a8f-4dc8-a543-7fc1c7afa5e9", "type": "concept", "title": "speech community"}], "description": "Students learn that dialects are rule-governed systems rather than broken versions of a standard."}, {"id": "08276db7-bd2e-4b82-bd9e-981b0679595d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and identity", "children": [{"id": "f9656ef7-3c88-40c3-8278-d265cc82862d", "type": "concept", "title": "indexicality"}, {"id": "3ed51d69-8866-4d5e-9349-4928851575f0", "type": "concept", "title": "style-shifting"}, {"id": "8987b79f-7d1d-4f3a-8b81-4e7db0f7458a", "type": "concept", "title": "African American English"}, {"id": "8b8521a9-3349-4a95-bea6-94175faacf7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Latinx English"}, {"id": "0915df83-991d-4cca-9f88-02bb904220a0", "type": "concept", "title": "genderlect"}, {"id": "d4d8c9a8-cafb-44c5-8263-fd21695832af", "type": "concept", "title": "community of practice"}], "description": "This subtopic explores how speakers use language to index race, ethnicity, gender, sexuality, age, and belonging."}, {"id": "99684089-e09f-402a-96d8-05cb11a4c3e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language attitudes and discrimination", "children": [{"id": "cc2afe62-3c4d-4c4d-90f4-66be31904d0a", "type": "concept", "title": "accent discrimination"}, {"id": "fc8bb385-8696-40f4-a41f-f1666731f7d1", "type": "concept", "title": "linguistic profiling"}, {"id": "3cabf3dd-5013-4ff5-b94f-8f453d9009a8", "type": "concept", "title": "standard language ideology"}, {"id": "3f7fa95d-1825-43e1-821c-7ecdcf7a5b53", "type": "concept", "title": "code-switching"}, {"id": "3218cf36-5a1c-4307-9367-a2b0626e148c", "type": "concept", "title": "translanguaging"}, {"id": "3ecb5414-ecc9-4667-90c2-40dce30b3b7a", "type": "concept", "title": "linguistic justice"}], "description": "Students examine accent bias, educational policy, linguistic profiling, and public debates about correctness."}]}, {"id": "b0beb672-223a-4f77-9021-40c7661059d3", "type": "topic", "title": "Writing Systems, Spelling, and Orthography", "description": "English spelling is historical, layered, and socially powerful. This unit explains why orthography works the way it does.", "children": [{"id": "267c1a93-4e35-4213-9558-48f6a0a42468", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alphabets and writing systems", "children": [{"id": "93324e3e-e8e4-4d53-8ca4-b6194f040a48", "type": "concept", "title": "alphabet"}, {"id": "adbc6d62-4948-499e-a92a-88ff9117c659", "type": "concept", "title": "grapheme"}, {"id": "1e9b3786-bb22-4201-9540-d8b54620c7d4", "type": "concept", "title": "digraph"}, {"id": "9a6ba288-9e22-414f-aad7-c0a0e70de1bd", "type": "concept", "title": "rune"}, {"id": "1a94c32a-c173-47b1-b2f1-debb2737dccd", "type": "concept", "title": "manuscript hand"}, {"id": "2c8184