Norman Conquest and multilingual England", "children": [{"id": "8567ef0d-c2c9-4fbf-ad9b-87392f3de484", "type": "concept", "title": "Norman Conquest"}, {"id": "f76e0efe-125c-4055-b70f-4feac0f0e643", "type": "concept", "title": "Anglo-Norman"}, {"id": "92e4ec6e-60a5-4bce-99ee-6f30e50a14cc", "type": "concept", "title": "trilingualism"}, {"id": "033fbc70-4dd6-48b4-bf67-238df228135f", "type": "concept", "title": "French loanword"}, {"id": "ad4afb6e-3c21-41ff-9f0e-d45a4740cb4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin"}, {"id": "a2924ef7-5194-4fa4-ab02-e465617f4474", "type": "concept", "title": "diglossia"}], "description": "Students learn how French, Latin, and English interacted in law, literature, church, and everyday life."}, {"id": "e7941a96-8e2f-4a0f-992b-9903d3bd917b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Middle English grammar and dialects", "children": [{"id": "b95bb4c1-d0f5-463a-87ae-9c223011b7bc", "type": "concept", "title": "inflectional leveling"}, {"id": "6354cb7d-87a0-46be-9057-3d64d0ff7eca", "type": "concept", "title": "case loss"}, {"id": "b48221a9-ac74-4bde-8b8b-0142a2b72ba1", "type": "concept", "title": "Chancery Standard"}, {"id": "fd29a09f-4fc5-499b-b370-ff3da44b786e", "type": "concept", "title": "dialect"}, {"id": "7a5cfbd2-66f9-4e62-8564-14b5d963e55a", "type": "concept", "title": "plural -s"}, {"id": "6e17a0b6-7f4a-4829-a95b-d00626097579", "type": "concept", "title": "word order"}], "description": "This subtopic examines the loss of many inflections and the rise of regional written forms."}, {"id": "852e03e6-7f66-4596-bb72-c1c397c438fd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Middle English texts", "children": [{"id": "693f0fa2-ce94-4ad6-a44b-335e8bd5993c", "type": "concept", "title": "Chaucer"}, {"id": "f6922714-8d41-4633-8f3c-48b1c8892b3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Piers Plowman"}, {"id": "899bab52-7e0f-43f3-ad10-83dd85766d71", "type": "concept", "title": "Sir Gawain and the Green Knight"}, {"id": "a60395fd-5360-4d39-ba96-c4e58ba4a90c", "type": "concept", "title": "manuscript"}, {"id": "0e46b3be-f27f-4fce-9599-65bf6e89c339", "type": "concept", "title": "scribe"}, {"id": "8ef667a5-9c28-4011-80eb-2ab2c4034bb2", "type": "concept", "title": "orthographic variation"}], "description": "Students read language history through major texts and manuscript culture."}]}, {"id": "74e17287-0ff7-4ffe-b8c6-bb5f90d87baa", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Modern English", "description": "Early Modern English links Shakespeare, print, standardization, colonial expansion, dictionary-making, and major sound change.", "children": [{"id": "c6389a55-37ff-46e7-b633-2f6edf11ea16", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Great Vowel Shift and pronunciation", "children": [{"id": "f905a2d7-4713-4e05-bef6-1702b1a9cd70", "type": "concept", "title": "Great Vowel Shift"}, {"id": "76164963-b2de-41b7-a12c-1e77856bc75f", "type": "concept", "title": "long vowel"}, {"id": "33215b31-9b50-4a48-81d2-fe902177cfdc", "type": "concept", "title": "spelling-pronunciation gap"}, {"id": "eacfae2d-73a4-496d-acd3-cca08eff9c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "orthoepy"}, {"id": "162813df-147e-4f1a-8d36-3667095fb80f", "type": "concept", "title": "rhoticity"}, {"id": "6ae0ccba-71b0-4443-a8b2-caa422cde06f", "type": "concept", "title": "pronunciation guide"}], "description": "Students learn why English spelling and pronunciation diverged and how sound change shaped modern English."}, {"id": "569c0c1a-d0d4-4dac-882a-b215850880e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Print, standardization, and dictionaries", "children": [{"id": "3125c521-225e-49f4-81ad-6064f7bcac3c", "type": "concept", "title": "printing press"}, {"id": "3da40032-c8f9-4dd0-90e8-6453775d8a8b", "type": "concept", "title": "standardization"}, {"id": "13ac4eba-5226-470a-a0dc-4168236526a8", "type": "concept", "title": "orthography"}, {"id": "387f11ca-b9c0-4875-a198-e0570325f59c", "type": "concept", "title": "dictionary"}, {"id": "fe513ad2-62c7-49b7-a308-5f32bbe5b7f6", "type": "concept", "title": "grammar book"}, {"id": "713a9d07-c564-4b60-8da5-6ddffcd757a3", "type": "concept", "title": "prescriptive tradition"}], "description": "This subtopic connects language change to print culture, education, grammars, dictionaries, and spelling norms."}, {"id": "58df485e-acb2-481c-89a2-931f1def374d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Shakespeare and Early Modern usage", "children": [{"id": "fcf3aa99-eb30-4798-a3ee-70b59c0f081a", "type": "concept", "title": "Shakespeare"}, {"id": "1921216a-7eeb-4782-9cb0-47b7f721e4ca", "type": "concept", "title": "neologism"}, {"id": "715decbe-18c2-49c2-9774-55f9d28f5dad", "type": "concept", "title": "thou / you"}, {"id": "e12d0f53-5aa8-4fb4-a02d-1fd4c48be945", "type": "concept", "title": "auxiliary do"}, {"id": "062e0d6a-02b0-4fd8-81de-939c8b0765ec", "type": "concept", "title": "relative pronoun"}, {"id": "0ec268db-3633-429b-9308-28d2f60e3b91", "type": "concept", "title": "Early Modern English"}], "description": "Students use literary language as evidence for grammar, vocabulary, rhetoric, and variation."}]}, {"id": "b7a336f1-829d-49ea-a924-6c3c03ec9fcf", "type": "topic", "title": "Modern English, Standardization, and Usage", "description": "This unit traces modern English through dictionaries, grammars, education, empire, mass media, and ongoing disputes about correctness.", "children": [{"id": "4a53c345-4527-4aaa-9271-c9754b16042d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Standard English and institutions", "children": [{"id": "4e42a339-9942-4417-810e-7e814c9c9d5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Standard English"}, {"id": "bd040e75-5e8b-4dde-8fce-9b89fe1cbc76", "type": "concept", "title": "Received Pronunciation"}, {"id": "60572136-d791-4614-bd31-a23f6b4f439b", "type": "concept", "title": "style guide"}, {"id": "cb22b007-f1c3-4d7f-ae3e-9727da529b73", "type": "concept", "title": "copyediting"}, {"id": "a3029dde-44b8-491a-9e15-a45d67ebbf1e", "type": "concept", "title": "education"}, {"id": "a703e25d-832b-4d59-a43f-a2f89357041a", "type": "concept", "title": "language academy debate"}], "description": "Students examine how schools, publishers, exams, dictionaries, and media enforce language norms."}, {"id": "bbda0c6d-808f-4412-a5bd-600629ec5064", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Usage controversies", "children": [{"id": "6b895b51-ba0b-4080-bb55-f69a28ff9bf7", "type": "concept", "title": "split infinitive"}, {"id": "477d6bae-f277-4361-a25e-3bafc8dde183", "type": "concept", "title": "singular they"}, {"id": "123507c9-b179-4a4a-8996-191e8b5dc57b", "type": "concept", "title": "who / whom"}, {"id": "bb1b1acb-a44b-48f5-b86a-be56cc9985ad", "type": "concept", "title": "double negative"}, {"id": "1b08fa1e-0eaf-493c-a248-1f0c094d317c", "type": "concept", "title": "passive voice"}, {"id": "6748486b-90e3-44f3-9186-97084638ebe0", "type": "concept", "title": "ending preposition"}], "description": "This subtopic treats familiar usage disputes as evidence for language ideology and change."}, {"id": "354ba0d8-5766-4fe7-bbd2-39fc8535dc32", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Corpus evidence and actual use", "children": [{"id": "5f299d22-8c5d-4df1-b995-8afb2ccebed3", "type": "concept", "title": "corpus linguistics"}, {"id": "4c59d65e-5738-47b8-9502-735327ea171d", "type": "concept", "title": "COCA"}, {"id": "d02bae14-1923-40c5-bfd5-cb6fe0d46025", "type": "concept", "title": "Google Ngram"}, {"id": "104c616c-70d9-4fcc-9952-accd7dffafff", "type": "concept", "title": "frequency"}, {"id": "72247bdc-e557-4e8a-bc43-482f8576996c", "type": "concept", "title": "collocation"}, {"id": "2371d0ef-f58c-47a4-b182-83e1ee4fd73b", "type": "concept", "title": "register"}], "description": "Students learn to compare prescriptions with real usage data."}]}, {"id": "2180e801-63c5-4bc2-a82f-b77e8158f36e", "type": "topic", "title": "World Englishes and Global English", "description": "English is no longer one national language. This unit studies English as a global, postcolonial, multilingual, and locally adapted language family.", "children": [{"id": "a910b16e-b9a9-4ef8-94f7-7e7d7bb4f438", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models of World Englishes", "children": [{"id": "2601a693-26ae-4b33-a662-48b293f9c17b", "type": "concept", "title": "Kachru's circles"}, {"id": "607eb3fd-7b66-417c-9c59-c6e616f99a0c", "type": "concept", "title": "inner circle"}, {"id": "f2be019f-ce7f-4399-9e58-d80ab7a2ebf6", "type": "concept", "title": "outer circle"}, {"id": "a56bf1bf-a0de-4226-8aff-924e5e906f56", "type": "concept", "title": "expanding circle"}, {"id": "2abc91b0-5acf-49d6-8e6a-7723ebc9b030", "type": "concept", "title": "pluricentric language"}, {"id": "caf3e60c-5e8f-448d-b0a4-bddf9d0c508d", "type": "concept", "title": "English as a lingua franca"}], "description": "Students learn frameworks for describing global English without treating all varieties as deviations from one center."}, {"id": "17ae70ce-3e51-4f1a-ba66-ad1ac30d76e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial and local Englishes", "children": [{"id": "df440912-4ba7-40f9-ab07-bd9fa6432c6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian English"}, {"id": "f7b59440-b363-459d-add3-b106313a2cb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Nigerian English"}, {"id": "c7fa2a93-d922-43eb-9c10-f08b3cb3edfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Singapore English"}, {"id": "70f4fefb-6246-4dd4-863c-57eefd144ffe", "type": "concept", "title": "Caribbean English"}, {"id": "046dd990-49a3-4c63-9a35-ce65436e6d00", "type": "concept", "title": "code-mixing"}, {"id": "e668fb33-ddf4-40d0-8061-d767b329dce5", "type": "concept", "title": "local norm"}], "description": "This subtopic examines English varieties shaped by colonial history, education, literature, and multilingual contact."}, {"id": "95dd6364-8742-4ab1-bb55-e9bf7ddd1718", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global English and power", "children": [{"id": "2e262321-d1b1-457e-9f8a-70728001d2b4", "type": "concept", "title": "linguistic imperialism"}, {"id": "3648595f-0450-4a01-9672-868fcc85417a", "type": "concept", "title": "language policy"}, {"id": "01eb43f7-b088-4737-91bd-e56881f51d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "accent prestige"}, {"id": "8dda4c3a-7d6f-4cb1-bca6-fcba15c017ad", "type": "concept", "title": "globalization"}, {"id": "4bacbaab-7fde-41b8-a7ee-16b3becfd5e3", "type": "concept", "title": "translation"}, {"id": "1aab6507-830d-490d-ab36-1eb89e712002", "type": "concept", "title": "language rights"}], "description": "Students analyze how English creates access, inequality, prestige, resistance, and identity."}]}, {"id": "8e393d85-21d5-450b-aefe-268eb51c0494", "type": "topic", "title": "Dialects, Variation, and Sociolinguistics", "description": "Sociolinguistics studies language variation as patterned, meaningful, and tied to identity, place, class, race, gender, and situation.", "children": [{"id": "7f702b76-e8a6-4d5e-9f5f-9bf750d49f7a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regional and social dialects", "children": [{"id": "1c5fad93-72df-4b09-832a-4d593939b9fe", "type": "concept", "title": "dialect"}, {"id": "e4b4c7ad-5437-4da2-be2b-4e838d4e2406", "type": "concept", "title": "idiolect"}, {"id": "eda4c80d-a07b-4480-b561-e04763de9785", "type": "concept", "title": "isogloss"}, {"id": "741b23b7-1c8d-4a8f-9c91-7c6e7691d092", "type": "concept", "title": "regional variation"}, {"id": "067a682c-2f53-4097-9abe-7cbb00312c56", "type": "concept", "title": "social class"}, {"id": "4ed2a600-8a8f-4dc8-a543-7fc1c7afa5e9", "type": "concept", "title": "speech community"}], "description": "Students learn that dialects are rule-governed systems rather than broken versions of a standard."}, {"id": "08276db7-bd2e-4b82-bd9e-981b0679595d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and identity", "children": [{"id": "f9656ef7-3c88-40c3-8278-d265cc82862d", "type": "concept", "title": "indexicality"}, {"id": "3ed51d69-8866-4d5e-9349-4928851575f0", "type": "concept", "title": "style-shifting"}, {"id": "8987b79f-7d1d-4f3a-8b81-4e7db0f7458a", "type": "concept", "title": "African American English"}, {"id": "8b8521a9-3349-4a95-bea6-94175faacf7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Latinx English"}, {"id": "0915df83-991d-4c